# a-defended-base — plan 20 A5's fixture: a world with a ROSTER in it.
#
# ⚠⚠ `the-ground` and `an-island` are worlds with nobody in them, and
# that is now load-bearing rather than incidental: the GL gate draws the
# entities for every fixture, so their `other == 0` says *no entity was
# drawn in a base with no roster* as well as *no drift in the ground*.
# This fixture is the other half — the same frame-filling grass, with
# one of everything standing on it.
#
# ⚠ The ground FILLS THE FRAME for `the-ground`'s reason: the clear
# colour is deliberately not a palette colour, so with no background in
# the shot every non-palette pixel is an ENTITY and the gate can make an
# exact, total claim —
#
#     every pixel is a palette colour, an entity colour, or the clear
#     colour, and nothing else
#
# — which is what `src/gl_gate.loft::gl_case_a_defended_base` asserts by
# summing the entity colours BY NAME and requiring the sum to be the
# whole of `unknown`.
#
# ⚠⚠ **THE PLAYER AT (0, 0) IS THE LANDMARK**, and it is at the centre
# of the frame on purpose.  A per-class pixel count is
# permutation-invariant (`@X078`), so the gate also asks WHERE the player
# landed — and a hover unit is drawn 0.4 m ABOVE its hex, which a camera
# looking down from 20 m displaces outward everywhere except at the
# centre.  At (0, 0) the displacement is 0.5 px, so the tolerance stays
# tight enough to catch a mirrored world.
#
# ⚠ Every entity is inside q ±8, r ±7 — the visible ground plane is
# about q ±11 by r ±10 at this camera, and a 6 m tower leans OUTWARD by
# a factor of 20/(20-6) as it rises.

echo a-defended-base: a base with one of everything standing in it

at 0 0 1

# ── The ground, painted past the frustum. ──
palette 5
drag -22 -14 22 -14
drag -22 -13 22 -13
drag -22 -12 22 -12
drag -22 -11 22 -11
drag -22 -10 22 -10
drag -22  -9 22  -9
drag -22  -8 22  -8
drag -22  -7 22  -7
drag -22  -6 22  -6
drag -22  -5 22  -5
drag -22  -4 22  -4
drag -22  -3 22  -3
drag -22  -2 22  -2
drag -22  -1 22  -1
drag -22   0 22   0
drag -22   1 22   1
drag -22   2 22   2
drag -22   3 22   3
drag -22   4 22   4
drag -22   5 22   5
drag -22   6 22   6
drag -22   7 22   7
drag -22   8 22   8
drag -22   9 22   9
drag -22  10 22  10
drag -22  11 22  11
drag -22  12 22  12
drag -22  13 22  13
drag -22  14 22  14

# ── The two towers.  One whole, one with its top taken off. ──
#
# ⚠ The pair is the point: `tower_has_top` is a SIMULATION fact (plan 17
# T2) and A4 made the drawing read it, so a frame with two towers and
# ONE top is the smallest world in which a renderer that drew the top
# from a second flag would still look right in half the picture.
#
# ⚠ Placed the editor's own way — `toggle_mode` into the marker layer,
# `cycle_kind` twice (spawn -> target -> tower), then a click each.  A
# `.keys` file has no "put a tower here" verb and deliberately does not:
# every command names an ACTION, so the script exercises the same
# `place_marker` dispatch the player does.
do toggle_mode
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
click  6  2
click  7 -4
do toggle_mode
marker  6  2 tower
marker  7 -4 tower
count markers 2 2

tower   7 -4 0 0.0 0.0 off
top     6  2 on
top     7 -4 off

# ── The player, parked at the centre and pointed EAST. ──
#
# ⚠ `drive` sets the destination without spending a tick, which is what
# gives a parked vehicle a FACING to draw — a bearing comes from the
# velocity (`@X067`) and a vehicle whose destination is its own hex has
# none.
park  0 0
drive 4 0
vehicle 0 0

# ── One crew member, pointed back at the core. ──
crew 4 4
send 0 0 0
roster 1 1
helper 0 4 4

# ── Four robots of three classes, walking east into the base. ──
#
# ⚠ Three classes rather than one, because `PARTS.md` § D8 makes a class
# *a row of DATA* — one shared colour would leave that unbuilt, and the
# gate reads each class as its own count.
place -6  0 robot 0
place -6  4 robot 0
place -6 -4 scout 0
place  0 -7 miner 5
count alive 4 4

# ── What the SOFTWARE gate can already see. ──
#
# ⚠ Not the GL measurements — `.keys` has no GL verb and deliberately
# does not (`@X076`).  These make the fixture a real scenario in its own
# right, so `validate.sh` checks the world before anything is drawn.
kind   0   0 grass
kind  23   0 sea
kind   0  15 sea
